Suppose a hard collision strikes one quark inside a meson. The quark begins to move away, but the flux tube stretches behind it. It may lose its kinetic energy and be pulled back. More often, once the stored field energy is large enough, the vacuum produces a new quark–anti-quark pair. The new anti-quark terminates the flux attached to the escaping quark, while the new quark terminates the flux attached to the original anti-quark. One stretched singlet becomes two shorter singlets, $(q\bar q)_{\mathrm{stretched}}
\longrightarrow
(q\bar q)+(q\bar q).$

